WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.830
Russell Brand hasn't exactly disappeared,
but it's been awhile since the lanky, whip-smart

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.750
British comedian has been given the movie
star treatment.

00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:12.680
His hilarious turn in Forgetting Sarah Marshall
made him seem well-positioned to own the silver

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.680
screen.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.320
Starring roles in more movies, a shot at headlining
the Arthur franchise reboot, and his own late-night

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.890
talk show followed, making it seem like Brand's
big personality and great comic talents were

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.199
in high demand — and would stay that way.

00:00:25.199 --> 00:00:28.869
But lately, he seems to have disappeared from
the multiplex altogether.

00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:33.270
Let's take a look at some of the reasons we
don't hear from Russell Brand so much anymore.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.520
Get him to the theaters

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:39.180
Brand earned raves as the breakout star of
Forgetting Sarah Marshall, a movie already

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.980
notable for launching Jason Segel's big-screen
writing and acting career.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:47.510
In the movie, Brand played the ostensible
villain, a pretentious British rock star named

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:48.510
Aldous Snow.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:52.329
But despite the character's many flaws, Brand
made Aldous entirely likable.

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:55.900
He was such an audience favorite in the film
that the producers put together Get Him to

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.720
the Greek, a spin-off movie centered on the
character.

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:03.880
Co-starring Jonah Hill as a record label employee
who has to get Snow to an important concert

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:06.409
at the historic Greek Theater in Los Angeles.

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:09.600
The movie didn't quite click as well with
audiences as Sarah Marshall did.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.400
That may have been a hint that moviegoers
might prefer Russell Brand in small doses

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.820
rather than a starring role.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.500
That certainly seemed to be the case for his
next big chance at the marquee.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:25.069
In 2011, Brand filmed a remake of the Oscar-nominated
1981 comedy Arthur, and in it Brand, who has

00:01:25.069 --> 00:01:28.109
a history of addiction, played a believable
sad drunk.

00:01:28.109 --> 00:01:33.049
But remakes have an uphill climb, and audiences
failed to turn out, slowing his film career

00:01:33.049 --> 00:01:34.399
momentum.

00:01:34.399 --> 00:01:35.399
Hosting debacle

00:01:35.399 --> 00:01:40.929
Among his first high-profile gigs in America
was his turn hosting the 2008 MTV Video Music

00:01:40.929 --> 00:01:41.929
Awards.

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:45.950
The show had a long history of using comedians
as hosts, but unfortunately, it didn't go

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:47.219
so well for Brand.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:52.139
While he was expected to make fun of sacred
cows, Brand went too far for a lot of people,

00:01:52.139 --> 00:01:56.710
mocking the Jonas Brothers' public pledges
to abstain from sex until marriage and the

00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.329
purity rings they wore to signify it.

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:03.840
Later in the show, fellow purity ring wearer
Jordin Sparks lashed out at him on-stage,

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.920
and this prompted Brand to awkwardly return
to the subject on-stage and declare, "I'd

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:10.521
like to take this opportunity to say, no one
ever have sex again.

00:02:10.521 --> 00:02:12.360
It's a mad idea.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.360
What a way to spend an evening!"

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:15.360
Voiceover wins

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.770
Maybe instead of watching him look and act
like a rock star, audiences would rather have

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.200
Brand disappear into a role — literally
— to the point where they can't even see

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.200
him.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.110
As proof, he's put together a pretty impressive
track record for voicing animated characters.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:33.500
He provided the voice of the Easter Bunny,
in the 2011 hit Hop, and the ancient, bumbling

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.620
Dr. Nefario in two Despicable Me movies.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.460
"I miss bein' evil."

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:43.180
And more recently, he was part of the cast
of the hit animated musical Trolls.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.260
Talk show takedown

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:49.290
The FX network got into the hot Russell Brand
game in the summer of 2012 by signing the

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.120
comedian on for a weekly late-night talk show
called Brand X.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:57.500
However, the series struggled to find a niche
in the crowded late-night comedy arena, and

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:59.900
Brand and producers changed formats a few
times.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:03.760
When the show debuted, it was little more
than a sounding board for Brand to speak,

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:06.080
joke, and riff about a political or social
issue.

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:09.710
"As you already know it's my propensity for
verbosity."

00:03:09.710 --> 00:03:14.370
Later on, he experimented with the format,
introducing celebrity interviews, an audience

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:17.710
advice segment, and even on-stage debates
with hate groups.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:22.010
But after roughly two dozen episodes over
the course of a year, Brand X was branded

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:24.510
a failure — and canceled.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:25.510
Sitcom sadness

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:29.600
FX got rid of Brand being himself on Brand
X so they could make a show where he pretended

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:30.620
to be himself.

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:34.770
In the same press release announcing Brand
X's cancellation, the network revealed it

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:39.180
had another project for the actor in store:
a single-camera documentary-style sitcom in

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:41.150
which he'd play a version of himself.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.340
The concept was likened to HBO's Curb Your
Enthusiasm.

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.650
Ironically, the network ultimately curbed
its own enthusiasm by declining to order the

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:49.770
show to series.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:52.040
"I slunk out."

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:52.700
"You slunk out?"

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:53.710
"Slunk.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:54.710
I slunk out."

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:55.710
"So you didn't do s---?

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:56.710
And you punked out.

00:03:56.710 --> 00:03:57.710
That's what the f--- happened, right?"

00:03:57.710 --> 00:03:58.710
"Yeah, I punked out.

00:03:58.710 --> 00:03:59.770
I punked out."

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:00.980
Romantic rows

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.560
Brand reached peak fame in October 2010 when
he married Katy Perry, one of the biggest

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:06.890
pop stars of the day.

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:11.041
It seemed like a bit of a mismatch: she was
a squeaky clean all-American pop star, while

00:04:11.041 --> 00:04:14.040
he was a former heroin addict and a stand-up
comedian.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:17.880
They were both, at the very least, cheeky
and good-looking, which was one of the things

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:22.590
that kept the marriage afloat for two years…until
Brand broke up with her via text message.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:26.380
Perry has spoken about the pain and sadness
of the divorce, and initially pinned the split

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.010
on their busy schedules.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:33.190
Brand later blamed his exit from the marriage
on Perry's commitment to what he called a

00:04:33.190 --> 00:04:36.540
"vapid, vacuous" celebrity lifestyle, but
he still came out of the whole ordeal looking

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:39.560
pretty bad, hurting his public image.

00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.570
The scrivener

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:45.070
In addition to all his other projects, Brand
has somehow found the time to write and publish

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:48.520
a whopping six books and kick off a career
making documentaries.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:53.450
Always a political comedian and a curious
guy, Brand's interest in politics have kind

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:57.020
of become his — well, Brand.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:01.840
He now spends most of his professional life
making documentary films about political causes.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.060
In 2015 he made The Emperor's New Clothes,
a film about the disappearing middle class,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:12.740
economic disparity, and the recent recession,
and before that he was hired by BBC3 to make

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:16.080
a documentary about the war on drugs, and
whether it had failed.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:20.540
This project had Brand going to other countries
to research their progressive drug policies,

00:05:20.540 --> 00:05:22.520
which was a subject close to Brand's heart.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:28.230
A recovering addict, he also made the 2012
BBC doc From Addiction to Recovery.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.230
Hitting the web

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:34.520
In between making full-length, in-depth documentary
films primarily about individual issues facing

00:05:34.520 --> 00:05:39.650
the poor and those living on the fringes of
society, Brand also has a YouTube series called

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:42.050
The Trews: True News with Russell Brand.

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:46.770
He's already made more than 380 episodes of
the series in which he delivers the news while

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:51.290
analyzing and making fun of it, then invites
on experts and pundits for further discussion.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:52.540
"Do you think I'm trivial?"

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:53.190
"Yes!"

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:56.080
"A minute ago you're having a go at me because
I wanted a revolution, now I'm trivial?"

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:57.080
"No, I'm asking...."

00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:01.580
Brand was never a monster movie star to begin
with, despite having some key memorable roles,

00:06:01.590 --> 00:06:05.810
but he's proven himself to be more of an ensemble
funnyman for films while approaching other

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:08.650
subjects that are more personally interesting
for him.

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:12.460
So while his days as a leading man in the
film industry seem to have ended as quickly

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:16.960
as they began, he's still got our ticket money
if and when he does sign up for that next

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.330
comedy sidekick flick.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:22.080
In the end, you can still get a full dose
of Russell Brand these days — you just need

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.230
to know where to look.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:24.230
Thanks for watching!

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:26.380
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:26.380 --> 00:06:28.560
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

